  "summary": "Quantitative density data and force-balance simulations now test mechanisms of colony separation, biofilm detachment, and gravitational effects on growth.",
  "synthesis": "Three recent preprints apply mechanistic physical models to microbial populations at different scales. Density measurements combined with a minimal biophysical model attribute Pseudomonas aeruginosa sibling colony separation to nutrient depletion rather than lethal inhibition. A geometry-dependent force-balance model tracks Staphylococcus epidermidis biofilm cell cluster size and shape as EPS adhesion is disrupted, benchmarked to 24-hour results. A third study modifies a microgravity cell-growth simulation with an improved growth functional form, isolating gravity-driven flow and sedimentation. The shared thread is the use of explicit physical constraints, including resource transport, mechanical force balance, and fluid transport, to explain colony-scale behavior. What is new is the emphasis on isolating a single physical driver in each model rather than fitting phenomenological descriptions alone.",
